; leviprobe.run -- start RIAPP on the Dell for an OWNER listening session and
; do nothing else. The sibling `lane_proof.run` is the scripted MIDI proof; this
; one exists because that proof is not what a Levi / keybed / mixer ear round
; needs: MIDISEND playing M4 and R5 into the app during a listening session
; floods the log with scripted notes and answers questions the owner is not
; asking. What the owner needs is the app, up, configured, and idle.
;
; Run with:  spike_server.py submit --spool /tmp/spike_spool_laptop --wait 90 \
;              --run-script RAM:LEVIPROBE.RUN
; NOT with --exec: --exec is not a shell on this lane (a redirect inside it
; returns rc=0 having written nothing), and ONE blocked command there wedges the
; agent's whole command path for ~90 s until an owner reboot. Measured, not
; guessed (llm-wiki 2026-10-02-dell-lane-bare-path-launch, 2026-10-09).
;
; COMMENTS ARE ';'. '#', 'REM' and '/*' are each EXECUTED as a command on this
; shell and each prints "object not found" -- and an unrecognised command STOPS
; the script, which is how an assumed `Unsetenv` once killed a run at line 25
; after 19 ms, leaving no app and no log at all.
;
; THIS FILE EXISTS IN GIT BECAUSE THE ORIGINAL DID NOT EXIST IN GIT AT ALL. The
; Dell's RAM: is wiped by every reboot (llm-wiki 2026-10-01-dell-deploy-abiv11;
; 2026-09-29-lane-reboot-recovery), and the launch script lived ONLY on RAM:.
; So every re-stage after a reboot silently depended on remembering the bytes
; that made it, and an app that will not start has no symptom -- it is the same
; silence as an app that starts and plays nothing. Standing rule: git holds
; everything load-bearing, RAM: and /tmp hold only regenerables.
;
; RECONSTRUCTED 2026-10-23, not copied: the original RAM: bytes are unrecoverable
; once RAM: has been wiped. The env block is `lane_proof.run`'s, which IS in git;
; the launch form is the ledger's (P-FX1: "launched from a backgrounded
; LEVIPROBE.RUN (`Run >NIL:`, not a bare prefix -- a bare prefix blocks and
; wedges the agent)"). If those two agree, so is this file -- and the checks
; below are what make that verifiable instead of assumed.
;
; WHAT PROVES THE START, IN ORDER: the ev log's first line carries
; `build=<sha>`, which says WHICH BYTES ran; `RUN`/`SONG load`/`TR PLAY` say it
; is playing; and `--ui-windows` afterwards MUST show exactly ONE RIAPP window,
; because a second instance alongside a live one contends and fails SILENTLY
; (llm-wiki 2026-10-02-one-requester-two-instances). Closing the previous
; window BEFORE this runs is a step for the caller, not a fix here -- this
; cannot see what is already up.
;
; SET NO REQUESTER, CLOSE NOTHING, NEVER `avail flush` (it can unlink the files
; being read), NEVER `quit` to a monitor (it kills the machine the proof runs on).

; ---- the env: where the two logs land, and what MIDI is wired to ----------
; RIAPP_* is read per line, so it must be set HERE: executed by the agent there
; is no other shell, and the first version of this script started nothing at all
; because the env was described as "from a shell on the Dell" instead of set.
setenv RIAPP_EVLOG RAM:
setenv RIAPP_LOG RAM:
setenv RIAPP_DEMO 0
setenv RIAPP_MIDI_IN riapp
; DEVOUT IS DELIBERATELY 0 HERE, WHERE lane_proof.run SETS IT TO 1. Not a
; style choice: since commit 272cf20 ("O1: notes and MMC on the Dell wire, and
; the init-order defect behind a silent devout") devout is no longer silent --
; it really puts the song's notes on the wire -- and RIAPP_MIDI_IN names the
; SAME camd cluster the sender writes into, because on one machine "the wire"
; IS the cluster (lane_proof.run's header: "this is CAMD into CAMD"). So an
; app with devout on and itself as its own receiver re-ingests everything it
; sends: measured on this file's first run, 8071 messages SEEN and 6886 PLAYED
; within 3.6 s of launch, `LEVI note` lines flooding, and RAM:RIAPP-EV.LOG
; growing ~56 KB per 12 s so that EVERY evidence pull came back
; `MISMATCH` -- the bulk protocol has no ranged read, so a file that grows
; mid-transfer is an unusable copy, and an unusable log is what makes the next
; "looks live but isn't" unfalsifiable rather than a grep.
;
; The outbound is still worth testing -- it is what R12/O0..O1 are about -- but
; it belongs in a session that asks about the outbound, not in one that asks
; whether a keybed or a mixer mute works. lane_proof.run keeps DEVOUT 1 for
; exactly that reason.
; CLKOUT IS 0 HERE FOR THE SAME REASON AS DEVOUT. With DEVOUT 0 the inbound
; still counted ~300 messages/s and RAM:RIAPP-EV.LOG grew ~3 KB/s -- one
; `NOTEIN played=...seen=` log line per inbound message -- because the sender
; is enabled by `clk_out || dev_out`, so a clock out on the same camd cluster
; the app reads from is the app clocking ITSELF. The rate was ~300 messages/s,
; which is NOT the measured 56.0001 intervals/s of R12 N3, so the clock is a
; CANDIDATE for the traffic and not a proven cause: this file's next run shows
; whether the counter stops climbing with both flags off, and if it does not,
; the sender is something else and this explanation is wrong.
;
; The cost is not cosmetic either way: ~3 KB/s of ev log, one `NOTEIN played=
; ...seen=` line per inbound message, is ~1 MB over a five-minute owner
; session, which is what turns an evidence pull into a MISMATCH (the bulk
; protocol has no ranged read) and an unbounded log into a standing
; contribution to wedging the guest.
;
; A clock-out round is a different session with a different question -- lane
; proof keeps both flags on. Probed: with DEVOUT 0 and CLKOUT 1 the counter
; climbed to seen=5249 in 17.6 s; with both off it is what this file's next
; run either does or does not show.
setenv RIAPP_MIDI_DEVOUT 0
setenv RIAPP_MIDI_CLKOUT 0
setenv RIAPP_MIDI_LATMS 8
; RIAPP_MIDI_NOTECH is deliberately NOT set -- unset is what makes melodic
; output follow the Leviasynth channel. It is absent in a bare `setenv` listing,
; and there is no Unsetenv on this shell to "unset" it with.

; ---- the listener goes first and in the background ------------------------
; Same reason as lane_proof.run: it must be running BEFORE the app starts and
; must outlive the session, so a note that arrives while nothing listens has
; somewhere to go. Staged name is MIDISEND.V11 because it is an ABIv11 build --
; calling it RAM:MIDISEND once gave "object not found" and skipped half a proof
; by a name. If it is not in RAM:, this line fails SILENTLY (Run >NIL:), so
; whether MIDI IN works at all is decided by the log line below, not by this one.
Run >NIL: RAM:MIDISEND.V11 riapp LISTEN 600 RAM:R6LISTEN.LOG

; ---- the app: backgrounded, or the agent's command path wedges ------------
; `Run` is asynchronous in AmigaShell; a bare prefix BLOCKS. >NIL: because a
; requester that inherits the agent's stdout is what the wedge costs.
Run >NIL: RAM:RIAPP.M4

; The window takes ~11-15 s to open and each Wait returns in ~1.06-1.14 s; ten
; Waits is what the click harness settled on. Fifteen here, and the CALLER still
; has to check --ui-windows for exactly one RIAPP window: a Wait is not proof.
Wait 15
